1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,250
Before the story, a word about sound.

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:21,604
Light is easy to stop. Close a door and the room behind it is gone. Turn a corner
and the street behind you is gone. Shut your eyes and everything is gone.

3
00:00:21,654 --> 00:00:27,630
Sound is not like that. Sound bends round the corner and comes under the door.

4
00:00:30,801 --> 00:00:42,955
You cannot make out the words. But you know there is a person, and whether they are
calm, and roughly how far away. The door stopped the light.

5
00:00:43,005 --> 00:00:45,270
The sound it only softened.

6
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:55,810
Sound almost always means that something just happened. A release, or a collision.
Energy went somewhere.

7
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:03,450
A firecracker is a release. A footstep is a collision, the smallest kind, repeated.

8
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,828
Nobody can walk silently.

9
00:01:07,878 --> 00:01:20,121
Every step is a small impact, and every impact makes a sound, and the sound tells
you where the person is, how heavy, how fast, and whether they are coming toward
you.

10
00:01:20,171 --> 00:01:28,510
Footsteps run through this whole series. You will hear them at a door in Shiloh, and
on the way to two pillars in Gaza.

11
00:01:30,186 --> 00:01:36,890
And sound comes back. Clap your hands in a stairwell and the stairwell answers.

12
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:49,730
Some blind people learn to use that on purpose, and all of them use it without
meaning to. A room sounds its size. A wall sounds like a wall before you reach it.

13
00:01:51,478 --> 00:02:02,361
God made light and called it good. He made sound too, and a blind man will tell you
he is grateful for every one of these properties, every day.

14
00:02:02,411 --> 00:02:03,550
Brian puts it this way.

15
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:10,640
[Brian] Ask a room full of blind people whether they would give up their hearing to
get their sight back. More than you could imagine would not.

16
00:02:10,690 --> 00:02:17,970
[Brian] I have wrestled with this one more than once. And while I'm not much of a
talker, I am a good listener, so I don't really know which I would choose myself.

17
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:22,890
One small story, from Brian, and then the hard part.

18
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:32,033
[Brian] I was pushing Barb in a wheelchair through a hallway at the VA, in a crowd,
when a man passed us the other way. I knew he was blind before anyone said so.

19
00:02:32,083 --> 00:02:39,427
[Brian] I heard his cane. I worked for years in a place full of blind people, and
that is a sound you do not forget.

20
00:02:39,477 --> 00:02:47,877
[Brian] He and his escort went on past us, twenty feet or so, and I heard him say he
needed a bus stop, because he had an eye appointment at a clinic a few blocks away.

21
00:02:47,927 --> 00:02:57,491
[Brian] A few blocks is a long way for a blind man who has not been taught the
route. I did not turn round. I did not have to.

22
00:02:57,541 --> 00:03:02,706
[Brian] We got our car and found him, right at the bus stop he had named, the one I
had heard from twenty feet away in a crowded hallway, and we got him where he needed
to go.

23
00:03:02,756 --> 00:03:10,686
[Brian] None of that came from a special gift. It is what hearing becomes when it is
the sense you live by. Sighted people filter most of what they hear out.

24
00:03:10,736 --> 00:03:12,830
[Brian] I cannot afford to, and I would not want to.

25
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:26,619
Hold that in mind, because it cuts both ways. The hearing that picked a cane out of
a crowd is the same hearing that cannot be shut.

26
00:03:26,669 --> 00:03:38,084
Sound has one more property, and it is the one this episode is about. You can close
your eyes. You cannot close your ears.

27
00:03:38,134 --> 00:03:45,190
Sound comes in whether you want it or not, and some sounds you would give almost
anything not to hear.

28
00:04:00,240 --> 00:04:10,910
Those are the easy ones. Brian has a few more of those, and they are not funny. Here
is Mister Scaredy Cat again. The voice is borrowed. The words are Brian's.

29
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:19,257
[Brian] I'm glad I didn't have to record myself for this. It is going to be a hard
one to talk about. There are sounds you cannot unhear.

30
00:04:19,307 --> 00:04:27,721
[Brian] I heard my dog cry out in pain when he jumped off the deck and tore his ACL.
He went blind from the knee surgery that followed, which I thought was a little
unfair.

31
00:04:27,771 --> 00:04:37,280
[Brian] I heard my doctor curse while he was operating on my eye. That was the last
day I had any usable vision. You cannot look away from a sound and pretend it is not
there.

32
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:46,683
[Brian] A sighted person can turn their head. The sound comes in anyway, and it
stays. Those two are the extremes, and I picked them because they make the point.

33
00:04:46,733 --> 00:04:56,116
[Brian] Most of the sounds I would rather not have heard are quieter than that, and
they are not mine. They are somebody else's pain. Strangely, it is seldom a blind
person's.

34
00:04:56,166 --> 00:05:02,991
[Brian] That is the one kind of trouble I know what to do with. It is everyone else,
and the sound of something I cannot fix.

35
00:05:03,041 --> 00:05:07,092
[Brian] That belongs to another episode in this series, so I will leave it there.

36
00:05:07,142 --> 00:05:12,630
[Brian] I mention it now only because the worst sound in this story is not the
king's own pain either.

37
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:27,944
A sound that stays. Hold on to that. People say "the last thing he saw" as if sight
were the only sense that leaves a mark. It is not.

38
00:05:27,994 --> 00:05:42,665
A sound lasts as long, and it gets in where a sight cannot. This is the story of a
man whose last sight was chosen for him, and arranged so that it would stay.

39
00:05:42,715 --> 00:05:47,290
It is also, if you listen for it, the story of what he heard.

40
00:05:52,340 --> 00:06:02,420
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

41
00:06:02,470 --> 00:06:11,522
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

42
00:06:11,572 --> 00:06:14,530
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

43
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:27,833
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

44
00:06:27,883 --> 00:06:43,790
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

45
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:49,686
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

46
00:06:49,736 --> 00:07:02,245
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

47
00:07:02,295 --> 00:07:09,530
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

48
00:07:10,580 --> 00:07:23,017
First, where we are. Jerusalem, about six hundred years before Christ, and the last
king who will ever reign in it. His name is Zedekiah.

49
00:07:23,067 --> 00:07:32,572
He was put on the throne by the king of Babylon, at twenty-one, after Babylon had
carried the previous king away. He reigned eleven years.

50
00:07:32,622 --> 00:07:44,113
He rebelled, against the repeated warnings of the prophet Jeremiah, who was in the
city the whole time, and for part of it in a cistern. Babylon came back.

51
00:07:44,163 --> 00:07:57,295
The siege lasted a year and a half. By the end the people were starving. Where this
is. Stand in Jerusalem and face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

52
00:07:57,345 --> 00:08:08,298
Jericho, where Bartimaeus sat in our second episode, is about six hours' walk
downhill at two o'clock. That is where this king was caught.

53
00:08:08,348 --> 00:08:17,100
Straight ahead at twelve o'clock, past Damascus, up the long valley between the
Lebanon mountains, is Riblah, in the land of Hamath,

54
00:08:17,150 --> 00:08:20,500
on the great road between this country and Babylon.

55
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:27,752
It is about two to three weeks' walk. The king of Babylon stayed there while his
armies worked in the west.

56
00:08:27,802 --> 00:08:38,320
And Babylon itself is beyond that again: the road bends east along the river
Euphrates and comes down to the city, another two months on foot.

57
00:08:38,370 --> 00:08:51,810
Jerusalem, Riblah, Babylon. Six hours to be caught, three weeks to be sentenced, two
months to the prison. Keep those three in order.

58
00:08:55,860 --> 00:09:06,610
The story is told three times in Scripture, in nearly the same words each time. We
will read it from Second Kings, chapter 25, verses 1 to 7.

59
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:20,801
[Reader] So in the ninth year of Zedekiah’s reign, on the tenth day of the tenth
month, Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon marched against Jerusalem with his entire
army.

60
00:09:20,851 --> 00:09:30,946
[Reader] They encamped outside the city and built a siege wall all around it. And
the city was kept under siege until King Zedekiah’s eleventh year.

61
00:09:30,996 --> 00:09:38,852
[Reader] By the ninth day of the fourth month, the famine in the city was so severe
that the people of the land had no food.

62
00:09:38,902 --> 00:09:44,198
[Reader] Then the city was breached; and though the Chaldeans had surrounded the
city,

63
00:09:44,248 --> 00:09:50,932
[Reader] all the men of war fled by night by way of the gate between the two walls
near the king’s garden.

64
00:09:50,982 --> 00:10:01,859
[Reader] They headed toward the Arabah, but the army of the Chaldeans pursued the
king and overtook him in the plains of Jericho, and his whole army deserted him.

65
00:10:01,909 --> 00:10:09,558
[Reader] The Chaldeans seized the king and brought him up to the king of Babylon at
Riblah, where they pronounced judgment on him.

66
00:10:09,608 --> 00:10:19,690
[Reader] And they slaughtered the sons of Zedekiah before his eyes. Then they put
out his eyes, bound him with bronze shackles, and took him to Babylon.

67
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:30,680
One verse. The text says it and moves on. We are not going to move on quite so
quickly, but we are going to be careful.

68
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:35,490
Nothing in this episode will be described that the text does not describe.

69
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:47,652
Before the end, it is worth knowing what kind of man this was, because the book of
Jeremiah tells us, and it is not what you might expect.

70
00:10:47,702 --> 00:10:54,872
Zedekiah was not a wicked king in the mold of the ones before him. He was a
frightened one.

71
00:10:54,922 --> 00:11:05,891
Three times during the siege he sent for Jeremiah in secret, and asked the same
question each time: is there a word from the LORD?

72
00:11:05,941 --> 00:11:18,195
There was, and it was always the same word. The last time, he met the prophet at a
side entrance of the Temple and swore an oath to protect him.

73
00:11:18,245 --> 00:11:21,690
Jeremiah chapter 38, verses 14 to 17.

74
00:11:22,240 --> 00:11:30,299
[Reader] Then King Zedekiah sent for Jeremiah the prophet and received him at the
third entrance to the house of the LORD.

75
00:11:30,349 --> 00:11:42,227
[Reader] “I am going to ask you something,” said the king to Jeremiah. “Do not hide
anything from me.” “If I tell you,” Jeremiah replied, “you will surely put me to
death.

76
00:11:42,277 --> 00:11:46,142
[Reader] And even if I give you advice, you will not listen to me.”

77
00:11:46,192 --> 00:11:55,544
[Reader] But King Zedekiah swore secretly to Jeremiah, “As surely as the LORD lives,
who has given us this life, I will not kill you,

78
00:11:55,594 --> 00:12:01,458
[Reader] nor will I deliver you into the hands of these men who are seeking your
life.”

79
00:12:01,508 --> 00:12:13,056
[Reader] Then Jeremiah said to Zedekiah, “This is what the LORD God of Hosts, the
God of Israel, says: ‘If you indeed surrender to the officers of the king of
Babylon,

80
00:12:13,106 --> 00:12:20,030
[Reader] then you will live, this city will not be burned down, and you and your
household will survive.

81
00:12:23,050 --> 00:12:31,686
Surrender, and you will live, and your household with you. He was told plainly how
to keep his sons.

82
00:12:31,736 --> 00:12:41,800
And the conversation ends with the king asking the prophet to keep it secret, on
pain of death. Jeremiah chapter 38, verse 24.

83
00:12:42,350 --> 00:12:49,740
[Reader] Then Zedekiah warned Jeremiah, “Do not let anyone know about this
conversation, or you will die.

84
00:12:50,790 --> 00:12:59,700
A man who kept asking for the word of the LORD and never once acted on it. That is
who went out through the gate.

85
00:13:01,010 --> 00:13:03,180
Imagine the night the wall was breached.

86
00:13:03,230 --> 00:13:11,037
No torches. A gate on the far side of the city from the breach, between two walls,
by the king's garden.

87
00:13:11,087 --> 00:13:22,003
The king and what is left of his army go through it in the dark and head down toward
the Jordan valley, the Arabah. Imagine what that is like for a sighted man.

88
00:13:22,053 --> 00:13:29,420
He cannot see the road either. He is listening, the whole way down, for horses
behind him.

89
00:13:31,470 --> 00:13:38,292
They caught him in the plains of Jericho, at dawn, the old dictionary says. His army
scattered.

90
00:13:38,342 --> 00:13:44,940
And they took him north, not to Babylon yet, but to Riblah, to the king of Babylon,
for sentence.

91
00:13:45,490 --> 00:13:57,272
We are not going to imagine Riblah. The text puts three things in order there, and
the order is the whole cruelty. First, his sons were killed in front of him.

92
00:13:57,322 --> 00:14:06,140
Then his eyes were put out. Then he was bound in bronze and taken away. Sons first,
so that he would see it.

93
00:14:06,190 --> 00:14:11,339
Eyes second, so that it would be the last thing he ever saw.

94
00:14:11,389 --> 00:14:22,603
Matthew Henry, three hundred years ago, saw exactly what was being done: the point
of the order was that the last sight his eyes ever took in would stay on him,

95
00:14:22,653 --> 00:14:25,771
as grief and horror, for as long as he lived.

96
00:14:25,821 --> 00:14:38,782
And of the blinding itself Henry said that Zedekiah ended his days before he ended
his life. One more thing, and the text does not say it, but a blind man knows it.

97
00:14:38,832 --> 00:14:48,000
Zedekiah's eyes were closed in that courtyard. His ears were not. Whatever he heard
there, he also carried to Babylon.

98
00:14:50,298 --> 00:14:55,234
Then the road. From Riblah to Babylon is a journey of weeks.

99
00:14:55,284 --> 00:15:06,069
He walked it, or was carried, in chains, blind, through country he had never seen,
into the greatest city in the world, which he would never see.

100
00:15:06,119 --> 00:15:16,218
Henry noticed the chains, and his point was a blind man's point: Zedekiah did not
need binding, his blindness bound him.

101
00:15:16,268 --> 00:15:19,753
They chained him anyway, for the disgrace of it.

102
00:15:19,803 --> 00:15:30,480
The last of the three accounts, in Jeremiah chapter 52, adds the one thing Kings
leaves out: he was kept in custody in Babylon until his dying day.

103
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,950
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

104
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:43,971
[Brian] Zedekiah brings back a lot of memories of pain and discomfort. I do not like
to talk about it, but if there is an episode for it, this is the one.

105
00:15:44,021 --> 00:15:47,769
[Brian] People do not realize how violent the treatment of a failing eye is.

106
00:15:47,819 --> 00:15:56,422
[Brian] I had nine months between the diagnosis and the end of my sight, and most of
that time was spent trying to save it. Laser surgeries to the back of the eye.

107
00:15:56,472 --> 00:16:06,110
[Brian] Scrapings on the surface. Replacing the fluid inside. Nitrogen bubbles to
try to hold parts in place. Four different bottles of eye drops three times a day.

108
00:16:06,160 --> 00:16:13,091
[Brian] Needles into it, more than once. Injections I knew were not working. Sorrow
in the voices tending to me.

109
00:16:13,141 --> 00:16:20,426
[Brian] There were days when I cried uncontrollably, not from sorrow or pain, but
just because my eyes would not stop watering.

110
00:16:20,476 --> 00:16:23,694
[Brian] There were days when it felt like ground-up glass was in my eyes.

111
00:16:23,744 --> 00:16:29,725
[Brian] And there is nothing to do with that kind of pain except get through the day
and hope the next one is better.

112
00:16:29,775 --> 00:16:36,597
[Brian] There are a great many more nerves in the eye than most people know, and
upsetting them was worse than anything else I have been through,

113
00:16:36,647 --> 00:16:38,553
[Brian] and that includes heart bypass surgery.

114
00:16:38,603 --> 00:16:46,299
[Brian] The last operation was the one where I heard the surgeon curse. I knew
before anyone told me. That was the last day I had any usable vision.

115
00:16:46,349 --> 00:16:55,858
[Brian] After that the eyes were not just useless, they were a source of pain with
no purpose left, and in the end I had both of them removed. People wince when I say
that.

116
00:16:55,908 --> 00:17:04,694
[Brian] It was one of the better decisions of my life. The pain went with them. I do
not have to worry about eye pain any more, and I am so grateful for that.

117
00:17:04,744 --> 00:17:09,467
[Brian] I am also grateful for everything I experienced and would not trade it for
much else.

118
00:17:09,517 --> 00:17:15,008
[Brian] It gives me a perspective that matches the ones I tend to and lets me meet
them where they are.

119
00:17:15,058 --> 00:17:22,660
[Brian] Many people who lose their sight still live with discomfort and pain long
after the sight is gone. So I know it could be worse.

120
00:17:22,710 --> 00:17:32,892
[Brian] And I notice that when I think back on those months, what comes first is not
what I saw, which was little. It is what I heard. The sorrow in those voices. The
surgeon.

121
00:17:32,942 --> 00:17:41,506
[Brian] The eyes are gone and the pain went with them. The sounds did not go
anywhere. And so I do not even want to think about what Zedekiah faced.

122
00:17:41,556 --> 00:17:51,199
[Brian] The last thing he saw was out of his worst nightmares, and then, with all of
that pain, he was made to walk from Riblah to Babylon, a journey of weeks,

123
00:17:51,249 --> 00:17:53,318
[Brian] to the prison where he would die.

124
00:17:53,368 --> 00:18:02,230
[Brian] Nobody was trying to save his eyes. Nobody gave him any treatment, any pain
relief, any comfort. The text says it in a single verse and moves on.

125
00:18:02,280 --> 00:18:04,570
[Brian] I cannot move on from it that quickly.

126
00:18:06,720 --> 00:18:17,369
Now the prophets, and a question the old books could not leave alone. Two prophets
had spoken about this king, and they seemed to contradict each other.

127
00:18:17,419 --> 00:18:26,110
Jeremiah, in the city, during the siege, told Zedekiah to his face what would
happen. Jeremiah chapter 34, verse 3.

128
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:34,973
[Reader] And you yourself will not escape his grasp, but will surely be captured and
delivered into his hand.

129
00:18:35,023 --> 00:18:42,510
[Reader] You will see the king of Babylon eye to eye and speak with him face to
face; and you will go to Babylon.

130
00:18:42,860 --> 00:18:49,307
You will see the king of Babylon, eye to eye. Now Ezekiel.

131
00:18:49,357 --> 00:19:00,842
He was already in Babylon, among the first exiles, and years before the siege God
had him act this out in front of them: dig through a wall at dusk,

132
00:19:00,892 --> 00:19:10,116
carry his baggage out through the hole, and cover his face so that he could not see
the land, while the exiles watched.

133
00:19:10,166 --> 00:19:15,930
Then God told them what it meant. Ezekiel chapter 12, verses 12 and 13.

134
00:19:16,190 --> 00:19:26,195
[Reader] And at dusk the prince among them will lift his bags to his shoulder and go
out. They will dig through the wall to bring him out.

135
00:19:26,245 --> 00:19:35,059
[Reader] He will cover his face so he cannot see the land. But I will spread My net
over him, and he will be caught in My snare.

136
00:19:35,109 --> 00:19:42,140
[Reader] I will bring him to Babylon, the land of the Chaldeans; yet he will not see
it, and there he will die.

137
00:19:43,312 --> 00:19:55,388
Years before it happened, in a foreign country, a man performed Zedekiah's blindness
in the street. He will go out at dusk. He will not see the land.

138
00:19:55,438 --> 00:20:09,572
He will be brought to Babylon, and he will not see it. Set that beside Jeremiah: he
will see the king of Babylon, eye to eye. Both cannot be true.

139
00:20:09,622 --> 00:20:14,900
Unless the blinding happens in between. Henry put it in one sentence.

140
00:20:15,450 --> 00:20:20,729
[Matthew Henry] By this two prophecies that seemed to contradict one another were
both fulfilled.

141
00:20:20,779 --> 00:20:29,017
[Matthew Henry] Jeremiah prophesied that Zedekiah should be brought to Babylon;
Ezekiel prophesied that he should not see Babylon.

142
00:20:29,067 --> 00:20:33,320
[Matthew Henry] He was brought thither, but, his eyes being put out, he did not see
it.

143
00:20:34,370 --> 00:20:43,302
At Riblah, he stood in front of the king of Babylon with his eyes. On the road after
Riblah, he had none.

144
00:20:43,352 --> 00:20:49,680
The order of events that was the cruelty was also, to the letter, what two prophets
had said.

145
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:02,447
Now the word. Each of these six stories has its own word for eyes that fail, and
across the series they make a chain.

146
00:21:02,497 --> 00:21:20,805
This story's word is the first link, and it is the hardest one: "ah-VAR." To blind.
The verb under "iv-VAIR," the plain Hebrew word for a blind man.

147
00:21:20,855 --> 00:21:27,427
Three times the Bible tells what was done to Zedekiah, and three times it uses this
verb.

148
00:21:27,477 --> 00:21:34,840
He is the only person in the Old Testament of whom it is used literally, of a thing
done to a man's eyes.

149
00:21:34,890 --> 00:21:47,002
Its other two uses are in the Law, and they are not about eyes at all. They are
about bribes. Exodus says a bribe blinds those who see.

150
00:21:47,052 --> 00:21:52,545
Deuteronomy says a bribe blinds the eyes of the wise.

151
00:21:52,595 --> 00:22:01,752
The word that describes what Babylon did to the king is the word Moses used for what
a gift in the wrong hand does to a judge.

152
00:22:01,802 --> 00:22:05,310
If you heard our Law series, you have met that idea before.

153
00:22:06,360 --> 00:22:19,907
And a second word, because it points forward. The shackles. All three accounts name
them, and the word, in its paired form, means two pieces of bronze.

154
00:22:19,957 --> 00:22:30,564
There is exactly one other man in the Bible bound in that word after being blinded.
He is Samson, four hundred years earlier, in Gaza.

155
00:22:30,614 --> 00:22:42,195
A king and a judge, the two men the Bible says were blinded by their enemies, both
led away in bronze. He is the last story in this series.

156
00:22:42,245 --> 00:22:46,230
We begin and end with the two men in chains.

157
00:22:48,460 --> 00:22:58,116
The Bible does not say how it was done, for Zedekiah or for Samson. The old books
guessed, and we will not go there.

158
00:22:58,166 --> 00:23:09,337
What the text does give is what came after, and it is not nothing. Jeremiah had made
Zedekiah a second promise in the same breath as the first.

159
00:23:09,387 --> 00:23:12,170
Jeremiah chapter 34, verses 4 and 5.

160
00:23:12,720 --> 00:23:26,709
[Reader] Yet hear the word of the LORD, O Zedekiah king of Judah. This is what the
LORD says concerning you: You will not die by the sword; you will die in peace.

161
00:23:26,759 --> 00:23:39,926
[Reader] As spices were burned for your fathers, the former kings who preceded you,
so people will burn spices for you and lament, ‘Alas, O master!’

162
00:23:39,976 --> 00:23:43,710
[Reader] For I Myself have spoken this word, declares the LORD.”

163
00:23:44,760 --> 00:23:54,043
He did not die by the sword. The record says he was kept in custody until the day of
his death, and nothing more.

164
00:23:54,093 --> 00:23:59,876
Whether they burned spices for him in Babylon, the text does not say.

165
00:23:59,926 --> 00:24:06,301
But the promise was made to his face, by the prophet he kept sending for in secret,

166
00:24:06,351 --> 00:24:13,190
and it was the one promise in all of this that he did not have to do anything to
receive.

167
00:24:14,240 --> 00:24:16,110
Brian, with the last word.

168
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:23,750
[Brian] I can't get past the last thing he saw. I am so blessed. The last thing I
remember seeing was my wife Barb.

169
00:24:26,500 --> 00:24:40,252
Next time, the first of a father and a son. A tent at evening, an old man who cannot
see, and the most familiar voice in the world, coming from the wrong place.

170
00:24:40,302 --> 00:24:42,450
Isaac, and the stolen blessing.

171
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:53,103
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

172
00:24:53,153 --> 00:25:04,022
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

173
00:25:04,072 --> 00:25:11,250
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

